WEBVTT

00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:52.690
Motorcycle racing, this is the magnet which year after year draws countless thousands

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:54.850
of spectators to circuits all over the world.

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:59.060
And this is Solitude, seen at the German Grand Prix.

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:03.230
one of the classic events counting towards the World Championships.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:08.580
Here you will find the big-time international riders.

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:13.100
Men like Rhodesian Gary Hocking, MV Works team rider,

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:16.740
after barely two seasons of European racing on private entries.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:20.220
New Zealander John Hempelman on an MZ two-stroke.

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:25.070
Japan's Kunimitsu Takahashi on one of the fabulous Honda Fours.

00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:29.630
And Jim Redman, also a Rhodesian and also on a Honda.

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:34.550
in every crowd you will find thousands of youngsters who perhaps like you

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:39.200
would like to become the hockings of tomorrow but apart from expensive machinery

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:44.900
what have riders like hocking and ubi-ali got that you haven't well maybe nothing but experience

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:50.440
but it's experience that has been gained the hard way for in motorcycle sport there are no

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:56.990
shortcuts to success and experience comes only with practice by riding in organized events as

00:01:56.990 --> 00:01:59.970
often as you can. Believe me, you can't let

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.960
to race on the public roads. What sort of events? Well, join a club and have a go at

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:12.510
trials for a start. Here you have the supreme example, the Scottish 60s trial, organised

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:19.020
by the Edinburgh and District Motor Club. Slow? Yes, but common or mountain mud and rocks

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:24.040
can teach even would-be racers a thing or three about machine control. And you'll find great

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:29.070
names like Geoff Duke, Sammy Miller and Ron Langston on many a shining national trials

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:29.390
trophy.

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:35.360
The bikes, pretty standard trials machinery, could be used for everyday transporter to pinch,

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:40.380
but right now they're off for six days of mountaineering among the Heather and the Haggis,

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:45.540
run by really experienced types like Jack Leslie, ex-road racer and trials rider.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:59.980
Trials are run in sections, and this one, Glen Ogle, is pretty easy, provided you prefer...

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.960
a gay throttle wrist and a well-balanced personality. Triumph Teamster Artie Ratliff,

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:11.700
a master of throttle control on the difficult section. And here's Artie's team mate Roy

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:24.760
Peplow, an ex-sine rider who won the Scottish in 59. How's that for bottom-end talk? Ladies

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:29.980
too, Jill Savage from Farnborough, Hampshire. Oh, hard luck, Jill. A few more...

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.230
lessons needed there I feel it's not quite so simple as the worksmen make it

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:44.300
look but let's move on to something much more difficult now at Grey Mare's Ridge

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:50.350
this'll sort some of them out throttle control balance path picking you have

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:53.010
to have mastered the lot to get up here without foot slogging

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:59.660
Ask Tommy McNabb Grey Mare's Ridge can teach you plenty including 57

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.890
ways of biting the dust. Peter Gurney, Peter Davey,

00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:10.780
Donald Whidden, Harry Ork, old Uncle Tom Copley.

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:17.530
But not all. When you can handle a machine like Market Gardener Gordon Blakeway,

00:04:17.770 --> 00:04:21.840
you're nesting in the top branches of the trials tree. For some,

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:25.580
trials riding is it. And if you decide this is your sport,

00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:27.810
there may be a works ride waiting for you.

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:29.980
But if you're...

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:36.210
hankering after pure speed your next step should be scrambling it's the mixture as before only much

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:42.710
faster so over to the british motocross grand prix at hawkston park to see what the world's

00:04:42.710 --> 00:04:48.360
finest rough riders have to teach us stan london and last gustafson of sweden on monarchs

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:53.540
don rickman and his matisse whose engine started life as a standard triumph tiger 100

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:59.690
and bsa pilot remy julien from france 25

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.860
stars from seven european countries ready to do battle for world championship points on a course

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:12.190
parched for lack of rain and played out through overuse here they come to line up engines running

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:17.700
in front of the elastic starting tape lightning reflexes mean everything in clutch starts like

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:28.290
this, they're off, streaking like jet-propelled sand to the neck of an hourglass.

00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:42.330
to study form now, chaps.

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:45.870
The leaders have already snatched a terrific advantage

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:46.930
over the backmarkers,

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:49.900
and championship runner-up Bill Nilsen of Sweden

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:53.020
hurls his hook varner ahead of Derek Rickman's Matisse

00:05:53.020 --> 00:05:55.680
as they storm Hawxton's one-in-three hill,

00:05:56.090 --> 00:05:57.710
with Don Rickman hard after them,

00:05:58.700 --> 00:05:59.980
leaving the rest to find the...

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.920
their way by faith, hope and astro-navigation. Luckily conditions aren't usually so bad even

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:16.140
in championship events. But here's an unscheduled hazard for John Burton and Rolf Tiblet, St.

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:21.500
John Ambulance Man, moving unlucky Derek Rickman, who's broken a leg. But even in races as

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:26.790
rugged as this, accidents are rare. Compared with road racing of course, speeds are relatively

00:06:26.790 --> 00:06:29.940
slow. It only feels like riding a pneumatic...

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:36.070
through the sound barrier much safer to master the dental arts of infighting

00:06:36.070 --> 00:06:39.530
cogswapping fast cornering and breaking on ball bearing surfaces

00:06:39.530 --> 00:06:46.030
where 999 times out of a thousand the worst you'll suffer is a few bruises and a deflated ego

00:06:46.030 --> 00:06:59.840
even with a matisse many a road racer would have got off and

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:04.830
around there but Don Rickman's lying second ahead of PSA's Arthur Lamkin and

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:12.750
champion Sten Lundin. Sorry about my dust dear, can't stop now and Gunnar Johansson of

00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:16.270
Sweden leads Belgians Roger van der Becken through the sand pits fighting to

00:07:16.270 --> 00:07:21.600
catch Lundin. Now battling Bill Nilsen has built up nearly a minute's lead and

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:25.000
that is what's basically an engine destined for a Swedish army's crappy.

00:07:25.910 --> 00:07:29.980
That's another advantage of scrambling. Relatively standard machinery can be

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:35.780
tuned to concert pitch if you've got the right brand of breath and even new models are far

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:40.160
cheaper than racers besides being much less prone to develop expensive noises

00:07:41.030 --> 00:07:45.880
provided you fit an efficient air filter just as well in the circumstances

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:05.160
But while our friend explores the undergrowth, let's watch Bill Nilsen's flawless technique

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:06.740
as he finishes his winning ride.

00:08:36.490 --> 00:08:38.390
How's that for mastery of man over machine?

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:43.240
Well, when you've learnt to control a motorcycle over this sort of ground and at this sort

00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:48.460
of speed, you'll be just about ready to try a drop of the hard stuff, real road circuit

00:08:48.460 --> 00:08:48.920
racing.

00:08:50.110 --> 00:08:52.930
Several circuit meetings are staged nowadays, especially for beginners.

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:57.900
And if you've learnt all you can from cars and scrambles, or even if you haven't taken

00:08:57.900 --> 00:08:59.980
a blind bit of notice of the advice I've given you.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:05.360
In the spring, your fancy will probably turn to thoughts of racing at some place like Cadwell Park,

00:09:05.960 --> 00:09:11.120
where, although entries are usually oversubscribed, a few novices are invariably given rides.

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:17.120
And there's often some ex-ace like Austin Monks around to help them.

00:09:18.050 --> 00:09:21.550
One thing you won't have learned elsewhere is the run and bump start technique.

00:09:22.660 --> 00:09:28.150
Fuel on, select bottom gear, back against compression, wait for it.

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:38.510
Heave away, drop the clutch and you're off, sooner or later.

00:09:50.990 --> 00:09:54.430
Cadwell is an ideal course for learning the rudiments of real road racing.

00:09:54.950 --> 00:09:59.970
With its wide variety of hills and bends, where expert use of brakes and gearbox can save seconds...

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:03.340
and with a longish straight where you can really get down to it.

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:07.480
What's more, it's narrow enough and has sufficient solid scenery around it

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:10.900
to demand a first-class passing technique, airline navigation.

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:17.140
Unlike some of the aerodrome circuits, where you can blithely point the front wheel in the general direction of next week

00:10:17.140 --> 00:10:21.000
with plenty of hay to mow before you're brought up short if you run out of road.

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:35.720
Cadwell packs all his charms into 1.3 miles, or sometimes a shade more.

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:47.480
Right, now you've made your racing debut,

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:51.840
and naturally you'd have won but for all those chaps under the counterworks machinery.

00:10:52.760 --> 00:10:56.360
So now let's see what else is needed to become a really top-notch privateer.

00:10:56.800 --> 00:10:59.970
And who better to show us than Bob McIntyre and his sponsor?

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:05.690
sir joe potts of bells hill glasgow joe's the backroom boy and business brain but there's no

00:11:05.690 --> 00:11:11.270
daylight between maxi is either particularly when it comes to tuning racing motorcycles and his

00:11:11.270 --> 00:11:18.760
advice is preparation one season's hardly over before bob's preparing for the next aided by ace

00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:25.790
mechanic tim flaming that means hours of precise patient painstaking work almost drudgery at times

00:11:25.790 --> 00:11:29.240
but with racing engines cleanliness is next to lustiness

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:34.020
And precision in preparation can mean the difference between success and failure.

00:11:38.940 --> 00:11:43.040
Few riders enjoy Bob's first-class facilities, like that Heenan and Fraud test brake.

00:11:43.860 --> 00:11:49.190
But fewer still could use them, or have earned them, as Mac has since his debut as a clubman in 1951.

00:11:50.230 --> 00:11:53.810
And he has all the inborn engineering ability so typical of the Scots.

00:11:54.540 --> 00:11:59.920
With the aid of the test brake, he reckons to be able to tune his AJS and Norton engines to suit any...

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:05.350
circuit you like to mention all winter and much of the summer too you'll find him hard at it

00:12:05.630 --> 00:12:10.750
squeezing every ounce of latent power out of his signal checking and rechecking their performance

00:12:10.750 --> 00:12:29.850
that's only a start power increases in the engine may alter the handle

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:35.080
on the track. So as soon as the model's nailed together, Bob's off to Alton Park with Alistair

00:12:35.080 --> 00:12:42.340
King to teach it the way round, to check carburation, gear ratios, suspension, the lot. It needs

00:12:42.340 --> 00:12:46.260
all the qualities of a test pilot able to diagnose the slightest fault in mid-flight,

00:12:46.930 --> 00:12:51.870
but with no ejector seat if things go wrong. Fasten your safety belts and we'll follow

00:12:51.870 --> 00:12:52.730
him round the course.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:20.570
It's up into second for Old Hall Corner, into third down the avenue, down to second for Cascades.

00:13:30.960 --> 00:13:34.020
It needs a special line engines to withstand this sort of treatment.

00:13:34.340 --> 00:13:38.830
And what's more, there's never a square inch of McIntyre in the breeze,

00:13:38.830 --> 00:13:40.690
a split second longer than need be.

00:13:42.410 --> 00:13:44.110
Into third, past the island.

00:13:50.820 --> 00:13:53.700
Then right down to first for that other petrol company's bed.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:02.780
the gears again until Max in top at the brow of the hill.

00:14:02.920 --> 00:14:22.120
Now to third for Nick O'Brook.

00:14:36.620 --> 00:14:41.800
Then up to top again, before changing down to second for Druids.

00:14:51.580 --> 00:14:53.640
Up to third, just before the footbridge.

00:14:54.060 --> 00:14:57.820
Then it's time to start changing down to first for Lodge Corner.

00:15:09.690 --> 00:15:13.070
Bob's ending his first lap now, so how would you like to have a go yourself?

00:15:13.910 --> 00:15:17.890
Keep an eye on the ref counter, don't go above 8,000 in any gear.

00:15:18.960 --> 00:15:20.860
And the best of British luck to you.

00:17:02.680 --> 00:17:05.100
Hmm, not at all bad for your first time round.

00:17:06.050 --> 00:17:08.630
With Bob, of course, this sort of thing's happening all the time.

00:17:09.170 --> 00:17:11.270
Checking his handiwork and testing your equipment.

00:17:11.970 --> 00:17:14.940
Always with a single aim in view, to win races.

00:17:15.580 --> 00:17:17.880
And above all, to win another tourist trophy.

00:17:18.810 --> 00:17:20.690
That one day may be your aim too.

00:17:21.110 --> 00:17:22.550
So let's go off to the Isle of Man.

00:17:26.360 --> 00:17:28.560
It's Wednesday, June 15, 1960.

00:17:29.260 --> 00:17:29.960
A new day.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:32.160
and a new look for the junior TT.

00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:35.260
Six laps of the mountain course instead of seven

00:17:35.260 --> 00:17:38.900
and riders away in graded pairs at 10 second intervals.

00:17:39.740 --> 00:17:41.660
But Bob and his AJS will start alone

00:17:41.660 --> 00:17:43.100
for Gary Hocking has scratched.

00:17:46.040 --> 00:17:47.910
He's off with nearly two and a half hours

00:17:47.910 --> 00:17:50.490
of fierce concentration to go before the chequered flag.

00:17:51.470 --> 00:17:53.650
Favorite John Surtey is on the MV4

00:17:53.650 --> 00:17:56.110
and young Mike Hale with his AJS.

00:17:56.840 --> 00:17:59.960
Soon they'll lift a narrow line of rubber all round the...

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:06.450
seven and three quarter miles course the right line let's see them late bob mcintyre on bray

00:18:06.450 --> 00:18:16.300
followed full chat by surtees on to quarterbridge and balacrane get offline on one corner and you'll

00:18:16.300 --> 00:18:21.000
be off it for the next half dozen but there's nothing wrong with surtees line at balacrane

00:18:21.000 --> 00:18:29.490
next streaks down from bergaro doing about 130 there and here's john hartle already ahead of

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:47.160
For laugh, Sertes is leading on the road, so Max A.J. still can't hold that MV4, and behind them, Hartle leads Minter and Bob Brown.

00:18:49.990 --> 00:18:55.450
Once past Ramsey, you're marching up that great high hill, 1,400 feet in five miles.

00:18:56.120 --> 00:18:59.980
Then over the mountain and eight miles further on, here's Sertes marching...

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:04.640
He's marching down again from Cates, sweeping through Craig Navarre for the second time.

00:19:07.930 --> 00:19:10.620
Hello, looks as though something may be wrong there.

00:19:11.050 --> 00:19:14.850
May be, for on the next lap Hartle cuts the lead by nearly 20 seconds,

00:19:16.060 --> 00:19:18.780
but the champion's still going strong at Hillbury the next time round,

00:19:19.250 --> 00:19:21.910
and straight past the pits to start his fourth circuit.

00:19:27.900 --> 00:19:29.840
S30 still leads, but he's slowing.

00:19:34.570 --> 00:19:42.490
Cadwell star George Catlin comes in to refuel and McIntyre also refuels having decided against a non-stop run.

00:19:44.100 --> 00:19:47.380
Off he goes down Bray Hill and through Quarterbridge in pursuit of Hartle.

00:19:54.680 --> 00:19:58.400
And at Braddon Hartle's already made up another second or so on 13s.

00:20:02.880 --> 00:20:07.620
Now watch the lines through Ramsey, John Hartle and Bob McIntyre.

00:20:13.210 --> 00:20:16.070
Past the waterworks, he's gaining on Surtees too.

00:20:17.480 --> 00:20:21.180
And round the gooseneck, braking and gear changing perfectly coordinated,

00:20:21.850 --> 00:20:23.050
not a split second wasted.

00:20:23.530 --> 00:20:25.390
That's the perfection of unending practice.

00:20:28.880 --> 00:20:29.980
And here's neat work by...

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:38.860
John Lewis and Eddie Crooks on Governor's awkward camber. Good for them, but not for John Surtees.

00:20:39.140 --> 00:20:44.980
That's ignoring the gremlins in the gearbox which are going to cost him the race. Last lap and Hartle's

00:20:44.980 --> 00:20:52.360
now firmly in the lead. At Solby, Mob's overhauling the champ. Can he catch him? Surtees MB's flagging now,

00:20:53.210 --> 00:20:55.990
but watch his impeccable style at the Ramsey hairpin.

00:21:00.800 --> 00:21:06.450
No, Mac can't do it. He's nearly 20 seconds behind Surtees as he lines up Hillbury for the last time.

00:21:13.020 --> 00:21:17.850
The chequered flag's ready and ironically Hartle scores his first Grand Prix win on the four

00:21:17.850 --> 00:21:24.350
within a few weeks of resigning from the MV team. So although all the midnight oil burnt in Belles

00:21:24.350 --> 00:21:29.960
Hill didn't bring Mac victory, to finish third, the first all-British combination of man and machine...

00:21:30.480 --> 00:21:36.330
after 226 miles of the toughest testing yet devised should be enough to satisfy most people

00:21:36.990 --> 00:21:41.930
except you of course nothing will satisfy sir but a works ride in a continental classic

00:21:41.930 --> 00:21:51.080
very well back to where we came in germany solitude the grand prix takahashi hocking

00:21:51.460 --> 00:21:57.920
hempelman all with works mounts which they've never raced here before last minute checks completed

00:21:57.920 --> 00:21:59.060
the tension mounts.

00:22:00.220 --> 00:22:04.060
Thirty seconds to go. Team managers and mechanics leave the starting grid.

00:22:06.860 --> 00:22:21.420
The flag's up. Hocking streaks into the lead.

00:22:22.060 --> 00:22:24.060
I shouldn't wonder if he learned that dodge from Surtees.

00:22:24.760 --> 00:22:27.240
He and little Carla Ubialli are pulling well away.

00:22:27.840 --> 00:22:29.960
But the field's after them like a swarm of bees in...

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:31.660
as they stream round Glemsec.

00:22:32.310 --> 00:22:33.450
Hello? Something's happened.

00:22:34.080 --> 00:22:34.360
Yes.

00:22:35.280 --> 00:22:37.000
Kaluubiali's slid off on a damp patch

00:22:37.000 --> 00:22:38.300
where mud's been swept away.

00:22:43.290 --> 00:22:45.230
And here's the order at Steinbach, say.

00:22:46.010 --> 00:22:46.710
Hocking, MV.

00:22:47.330 --> 00:22:48.270
Empleman, MZ.

00:22:48.850 --> 00:22:49.890
Dickie Dale, MZ.

00:22:50.320 --> 00:22:51.620
And Taviri, MV.

00:22:56.630 --> 00:22:58.490
Hocking holds his lead at Vahingham.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:07.730
Back of the field, Plucky Ubiall is in the hunt again. Windscreen hold and his left megaphone wearing a distinctly injured expression.

00:23:08.390 --> 00:23:13.520
Come the leaders through Sandberg. Order as before.

00:23:23.420 --> 00:23:28.800
Carlo, third from last in 22nd spot. He's really got to get on with the knitting after that furler.

00:23:34.060 --> 00:23:36.880
Gary's still romping away like a bankrupt from the bailiffs.

00:23:37.360 --> 00:23:40.460
He's already built up a 150-yard lead over his pursuers.

00:23:46.510 --> 00:23:49.530
Three-quarters of the way through lap two, it's still hocking in the lead,

00:23:50.140 --> 00:23:52.440
followed by Hampelman, Taviri and Dale,

00:23:52.830 --> 00:23:54.670
having a terrific scrap for second place.

00:23:57.410 --> 00:23:59.850
Nubial has already snatched back another eight places.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:01.300
to slip into 14th position.

00:24:02.160 --> 00:24:04.710
The race roars on.

00:24:05.670 --> 00:24:08.310
MV team manager Nella Pagani's keeping a watchful eye

00:24:08.310 --> 00:24:11.380
on things, and none better able to plot race strategies

00:24:11.380 --> 00:24:12.560
than this expert series.

00:24:15.400 --> 00:24:17.200
18 seconds lead, that's clear enough.

00:24:17.890 --> 00:24:19.550
But Honda's oriental hieroglyphics

00:24:19.550 --> 00:24:20.530
misfox the opposition.

00:24:24.820 --> 00:24:26.040
Six laps gone now.

00:24:26.730 --> 00:24:29.150
Empleman, Tiberi, and Dale are almost

00:24:29.150 --> 00:24:29.910
riding one another.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:36.460
machines at vyingen in the tear up for second place and now ubiari's opened up all the stops

00:24:36.460 --> 00:24:42.470
in a determined effort to move up into sixth berth watch tanaka and takahashi glance anxiously over

00:24:42.470 --> 00:24:52.810
their shoulders as carlo closes the gap discounting hocking at the start of lap seven is now dale

00:24:52.810 --> 00:24:58.980
heppelman and tabiri in second third and fourth places and ubiari is now in sixth place

00:25:00.640 --> 00:25:05.680
Hampleman, Taviri, Dale, and Ubialli is closing in on them rapidly.

00:25:10.160 --> 00:25:12.920
Now Dale has taken over from Taviri and is chasing Hampleman.

00:25:14.250 --> 00:25:16.950
But never say die, Carlo has them in his sights at last.

00:25:17.650 --> 00:25:22.690
All three dicing furiously, but they're sitting ducks for the tiny streak of lightning from Bergamo.

00:25:23.210 --> 00:25:26.760
Just before Vahingan, during a meteoric eighth lap,

00:25:27.460 --> 00:25:29.960
Ubialli passes all three of them to move into second place.

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:35.980
taking Tanaka with him into third position despite Ubi Ali's awe-inspiring ride

00:25:35.980 --> 00:25:41.560
during which he gobbled up 21 places and only 57 miles Hocking's lead was so

00:25:41.560 --> 00:25:46.020
great that with only three laps to go Ubi Ali was unable to make any impression

00:25:46.020 --> 00:25:52.480
on the lad from Bulawayo now within certain sight of victory and so young

00:25:52.480 --> 00:25:56.260
Gary Hocking wins his third Grand Prix in his first season as a works rider

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:06.900
a splendid second and Tanaka third. To Gary Hocking, the victor's laurels in a well-earned

00:26:06.900 --> 00:26:12.160
place in the hall of fame. To you, who dream of following in his wheel tracks, remember

00:26:12.160 --> 00:26:19.060
there are no shortcuts to success. Don't try to race before you can really ride. Try trials

00:26:19.060 --> 00:26:24.940
for a start. Then accelerate through scrambles to gain experience of wheel-to-wheel dicing

00:26:24.940 --> 00:26:29.790
which will prove invaluable in road racing and spend as much time on preparation.

00:26:30.090 --> 00:26:36.140
as you do in the saddle. Then, even if you don't win a Grand Prix and never land a works

00:26:36.140 --> 00:26:39.060
contract, you'll at least have a lot of fun trying.
